    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF DCF, component route, with the cyclical adaptations. CHRW is an asset-light freight broker/forwarder -- an operating company whose value is a free-cash-flow stream, so it routes to owner-FCF (no prior valuation or memory entry to be consistent with). CYCLICAL / MID-CYCLE FRAMING: gross revenue swings with freight rates (FY2022 $24.7bn -> FY2023 $17.6bn) and is a poor base; the FY2025 revenue decline (-8.4%) mixes the Europe Surface Transportation divestiture, lower ocean pricing and fuel surcharges. The build therefore compounds from FY2025 revenue but lets margins, not the FY2025 FCF, carry the judgment: year 1 is anchored to FY2026 guidance (operating income $964M-$1.04bn, reaffirmed at Q2 2026 with an assumed -3% freight market), which is a trough-volume year delivering management's 'mid-cycle' margin through Lean-AI cost-out (headcount -31% since 2022). The base then assumes a partial cycle recovery to a ~7.0-7.2% pre-SBC EBITDA margin on gross revenue, above the pre-2023 range (5.8-6.6% pre-SBC) to credit the structural cost-out, and does not extrapolate the FY2021-22 peak. FY2025 owner FCF ($687M) was flattered by working-capital release as revenue fell; H1 2026 OCF of only $104M shows the reverse as revenue grew 9%, so wc_capture is NEGATIVE (-8% of the revenue change: receivables exceed carrier payables). EBITDA margin paths are PRE-SBC (GAAP OI + D&A + SBC); FY2025 was 6.02% of revenue. Capex paths include capitalised software (FY2026 guide $65-75M). Cash tax: FY2025 cash taxes $95.0M on $722.5M pretax (13.2%); FY2026 ETR guide 18-20%, ramped to 21%.",
